---
import {
  BEACH_COMMONS_V16,
  COMPANIONS,
  FIRST_SEASON,
  OYSTER_SOURCES,
  OYSTER_STATS,
  PINTEREST_BOARDS,
  RADIUS_RESOURCES,
  SCOREBOARDS,
} from '../../lib/beach-commons-v16';

const canonical = BEACH_COMMONS_V16.url;
const socialImage = 'https://pointcast.xyz/images/og/b/0543.png';
const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `${canonical}#article`,
  headline: 'The Billion Little New Yorkers',
  alternativeHeadline: BEACH_COMMONS_V16.dek,
  description:
    'A reported verdict on Billion Oyster Project and a 25-mile El Segundo field guide for building a coastal commons without copying the wrong organism.',
  url: canonical,
  image: socialImage,
  datePublished: BEACH_COMMONS_V16.publishedAt,
  dateModified: BEACH_COMMONS_V16.publishedAt,
  creator: BEACH_COMMONS_V16.creators.map((creator) => ({
    '@type': creator.name.includes('Codex') ? 'Organization' : 'Person',
    name: creator.name,
  })),
  isPartOf: {
    '@type': 'CreativeWorkSeries',
    name: 'Beach Commons',
    url: 'https://pointcast.xyz/beach-commons',
  },
  about: [
    { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Billion Oyster Project' },
    { '@type': 'Place', name: 'New York Harbor' },
    { '@type': 'Place', name: 'El Segundo, California' },
  ],
};
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="index, follow, max-image-preview:large" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="The Billion Oyster Project did not simply restore oysters. It built a civic operating system. Beach Commons V16 reports what worked, what remains unfinished, and what the El Segundo coast can borrow."
    />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#163e48" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />
    <link rel="alternate" type="application/json" href="/beach-commons/v16.json" />
    <link rel="preload" as="image" href="/beach-commons/v16/assets/01-billion-little-new-yorkers.png" fetchpriority="high" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="PointCast" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="The Billion Little New Yorkers — Beach Commons V16" />
    <meta property="og:description" content={BEACH_COMMONS_V16.dek} />
    <meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />
    <meta property="og:image" content={socialImage} />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="The Billion Little New Yorkers — Beach Commons V16" />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={BEACH_COMMONS_V16.dek} />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content={socialImage} />
    <title>The Billion Little New Yorkers — Beach Commons V16 · PointCast</title>
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(structuredData)} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to the story</a>

    <nav class="nav" aria-label="Billion Little New Yorkers navigation">
      <a class="brand" href="/">PointCast</a>
      <span>Beach Commons · V16</span>
      <div>
        <a href="/beach-commons">Series</a>
        <a href="#verdict">Verdict</a>
        <a href="#radius">25-mile desk</a>
        <a href="/beach-commons/v16.json">JSON ↗</a>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <main id="main">
      <header class="hero">
        <div class="hero__image">
          <img
            src="/beach-commons/v16/assets/01-billion-little-new-yorkers.png"
            alt="An editorial cut-paper view of New York Harbor where students and volunteers work around a giant oyster-shaped civic map."
            width="1536"
            height="1024"
            fetchpriority="high"
          />
          <span class="hero__stamp" aria-hidden="true">16</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hero__copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">PointCast Field Study 016 · Reported commons</p>
          <h1>The Billion <em>Little</em> New Yorkers</h1>
          <p class="hero__dek">{BEACH_COMMONS_V16.dek}</p>
          <div class="byline">
            <span>Reporting + field translation by PointCast</span>
            <span>New York Harbor → El Segundo · July 2026</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="lede article-grid">
        <div class="margin-note">
          <strong>The short answer</strong>
          <p>A civic success. A real ecological achievement. An unfinished self-sustaining reef.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose prose--large">
          <p>
            New York has always been excellent at hiring organisms without benefits. Pigeons do atmosphere.
            Rats run night sanitation. Plane trees keep the avenues from looking entirely like a real-estate
            prospectus. And, in the harbor, oysters have been asked to filter water, build habitat, soften waves,
            host science class, receive restaurant shells, explain climate adaptation, and—by 2035—appear one
            billion strong.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is an unfair job description for a bivalve. It is also one of the most persuasive public projects
            in America.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="stat-ribbon" aria-label="Billion Oyster Project reported measures">
        {
          OYSTER_STATS.map((stat) => (
            <article>
              <strong>{stat.value}</strong>
              <span>{stat.label}</span>
              <p>{stat.note}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </section>

      <section class="article-grid" id="verdict">
        <aside class="section-marker">
          <span>01</span>
          <strong>The verdict</strong>
        </aside>
        <article class="prose">
          <h2>Yes, it worked. No, the slogan has not come true.</h2>
          <p>
            Billion Oyster Project’s arithmetic is easy to love. New York Harbor once held vast oyster reefs.
            Overharvesting, dredging, pollution, and shoreline transformation helped erase them. The project,
            launched from the New York Harbor School ecosystem, proposed a number big enough to reverse the
            direction of imagination: one billion oysters restored by 2035.
          </p>
          <p>
            The current public counter says roughly <strong>150 million oysters</strong>, three million pounds of
            restaurant shell collected, more than 40,000 students reached, and over 100 schools involved. A
            peer-reviewed lower Hudson study estimated 5.8 million live oysters across a six-acre restoration.
            At Jamaica Bay, researchers found adults surviving, growing, and reproducing—but did not observe the
            natural recruitment that would let the population casually take the operation off human hands.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the honest judgment has three columns. As a literal race to one billion, the project is behind the
            velocity its title implies. As ecological restoration, it has produced genuine oysters, habitat, data,
            and better methods at meaningful local scales. As a civic institution, it is a knockout.
          </p>
          <blockquote>
            The project’s most scalable product is not the oyster. It is the invitation to do consequential work
            in public.
          </blockquote>
          <p>
            A shell leaves a restaurant instead of a waste stream. It cures, receives juvenile oysters, moves
            through a classroom, arrives at a reef, and becomes a reason to test water, count life, learn a harbor,
            and come back. The oyster is not merely restored. It is assigned a social life.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <figure class="full-plate">
        <img
          src="/beach-commons/v16/assets/02-two-scoreboards.png"
          alt="Two illustrated scoreboards divide civic participation and living reef outcomes while people and marine life gather below."
          width="1536"
          height="1024"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption><span>Plate 02</span> Never let a crowded volunteer day impersonate a self-sustaining ecosystem.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section class="score-section">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">The useful audit</p>
          <h2>Every restoration commons needs two scoreboards.</h2>
          <p>
            One counts whether people can keep the institution alive. The other counts whether living systems can
            increasingly keep themselves alive. Neither score cancels the other.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="scoreboards">
          {
            SCOREBOARDS.map((board) => (
              <article>
                <div class="scoreboards__top">
                  <span>{board.number}</span>
                  <p>{board.verdict}</p>
                </div>
                <h3>{board.title}</h3>
                <p>{board.summary}</p>
                <ul>
                  {board.measures.map((measure) => <li>{measure}</li>)}
                </ul>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="article-grid">
        <aside class="section-marker">
          <span>02</span>
          <strong>The correction</strong>
        </aside>
        <article class="prose">
          <h2>Oysters are not a municipal alibi.</h2>
          <p>
            The charming version of the story says an adult oyster can filter many gallons in a day, so simply add
            oysters until the harbor becomes clear. The adult version notices pipes. New York’s great improvements
            in harbor water quality came through wastewater treatment, regulation, infrastructure, and decades of
            monitoring. Combined sewer overflows still put stormwater and sewage into waterways during rain.
          </p>
          <p>
            Reefs matter. They add habitat complexity, filter particles, create nursery ground, and may contribute
            to shoreline resilience. But a reef is not permission to avoid the treatment plant, the overflow plan,
            the budget hearing, or the public dashboard. When a charismatic organism is made responsible for a
            city’s metabolism, the organism gets the press release and the pipe escapes the meeting.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is another reason Billion Oyster Project succeeds. Its education work can turn a magical filter
            story into something harder and better: a generation that knows a harbor is biological and
            infrastructural at once.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <section class="pullout">
        <p>For El Segundo, the instruction is simple:</p>
        <h2>Steal the operating system.<br />Do not steal the organism.</h2>
      </section>

      <section class="article-grid">
        <aside class="section-marker">
          <span>03</span>
          <strong>Pacific translation</strong>
        </aside>
        <article class="prose">
          <h2>The West Coast remake should not be “Billion Oysters, but by LAX.”</h2>
          <p>
            New York Harbor is an estuary with a particular history, salinity, substrate, shoreline, regulatory
            world, and oyster ecology. Dockweiler is an exposed sandy Pacific beach beside an airport, a wastewater
            plant, remnant dune habitat, a bike path, fire rings, and astonishing civic weirdness. A copy-paste
            reef would confuse a mascot with a method.
          </p>
          <p>
            The method is more interesting anyway. Pick a local living process. Build a chain of custody around
            it. Give a first-timer real but bounded work. Connect school, science, maintenance, agency, and public
            story. Publish both scoreboards. Return next season.
          </p>
          <p>
            Here, the most legible foundation species may be coastal buckwheat supporting the El Segundo blue
            butterfly. The most urgent civic metabolism may be wastewater and recycled water. The most visible
            protection work may be dune fencing, sediment capture, native planting, and careful access. The most
            useful public ritual may be monitoring—not moving nature around, but becoming a reliable witness to it.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <figure class="full-plate full-plate--map">
        <img
          src="/beach-commons/v16/assets/03-el-segundo-radius.png"
          alt="An illustrated coastal map radiates from El Segundo toward dunes, wetlands, an aquarium, a wastewater plant, water monitoring, and the working harbor."
          width="1536"
          height="1024"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption><span>Plate 03</span> Twenty-five miles is not a brand territory. It is a radius of existing teachers.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section class="radius" id="radius">
        <div class="radius__head">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">The El Segundo 25-mile desk</p>
            <h2>Eight doors already open.</h2>
          </div>
          <p>
            A community resource, not a takeover plan. Distances are broad orientation bands from El Segundo,
            not precise travel measurements. Check current access, programs, and volunteer requirements with each
            organization.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="filters" aria-label="Filter the community resource desk">
          <div>
            <span>Distance</span>
            <button class="is-active" type="button" data-radius="all">All</button>
            <button type="button" data-radius="near">0–5 mi</button>
            <button type="button" data-radius="middle">5–12 mi</button>
            <button type="button" data-radius="far">12–25 mi</button>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span>Useful work</span>
            <button class="is-active" type="button" data-work="all">All</button>
            <button type="button" data-work="habitat">Habitat</button>
            <button type="button" data-work="water">Water</button>
            <button type="button" data-work="learning">Learning</button>
            <button type="button" data-work="watching">Watching</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="filter-status" aria-live="polite">Showing all 8 resources.</p>
        <div class="resource-grid">
          {
            RADIUS_RESOURCES.map((resource, index) => (
              <article
                class="resource"
                data-resource
                data-band={resource.band}
                data-work={resource.work.join(' ')}
              >
                <div class="resource__meta">
                  <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                  <span>{resource.distance}</span>
                </div>
                <p class="resource__org">{resource.organization}</p>
                <h3>{resource.title}</h3>
                <p>{resource.invitation}</p>
                {resource.caution && <p class="resource__caution">{resource.caution}</p>}
                <div class="resource__tags">
                  {resource.work.map((work) => <span>{work}</span>)}
                </div>
                <a href={resource.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open current resource ↗</a>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="season">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">A first season that requires no pretend nonprofit</p>
          <h2>Four weeks. Existing partners. One public receipt.</h2>
          <p>
            This is an editorial rehearsal, not an announced program. It proposes observation and partnership
            before intervention; no collecting, planting, moving habitat, or unsanctioned monitoring.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ol>
          {
            FIRST_SEASON.map((step) => (
              <li>
                <span>{step.week}</span>
                <div>
                  <h3>{step.title}</h3>
                  <p>{step.action}</p>
                </div>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="article-grid">
        <aside class="section-marker">
          <span>04</span>
          <strong>What to copy</strong>
        </aside>
        <article class="prose">
          <h2>A good commons makes the work visible enough to desire.</h2>
          <p>
            Billion Oyster Project has a genius for intermediate objects: shell piles, reef cages, monitoring
            stations, spat-on-shell, boats, gloves, clipboards, countable oysters. The work has texture. You can see
            where your hand enters the system. “Awareness” becomes less necessary because the object itself carries
            the explanation.
          </p>
          <p>
            El Segundo has intermediate objects too: a dune fence catching sand; coastal buckwheat with a butterfly
            story attached; a water sample; an odor log; a tank at the Roundhouse; a shoreline survey; a student
            moving from aquarium volunteer to harbor science. Beach Commons should make these chains pleasurable
            to follow without pretending to own them.
          </p>
          <p>
            The local opportunity is not a giant new organization. It is a beautiful public index of the work
            already underway, plus seasonal occasions when people can see how the parts connect. One field trip
            should lead naturally to a second. One organization’s exit door should point toward another’s entrance.
            The commons is the connective tissue.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <section class="companions" id="companions">
        <div class="companions__head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Two listening rooms</p>
          <h2>Harbor shift / Pacific radius</h2>
          <p>Private Spotify playlists created as companions to this edition.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="companion-grid">
          {
            COMPANIONS.map((companion, index) => (
              <a href={companion.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                <span>0{index + 1} · {companion.kind}</span>
                <h3>{companion.title}</h3>
                <p>{companion.note}</p>
                <strong>Open in Spotify ↗</strong>
              </a>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <div class="visual-companions">
          <div class="visual-companions__head">
            <p class="eyebrow">Two visual rooms</p>
            <h3>Forty field pins, evenly split.</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="visual-companions__grid">
            {
              PINTEREST_BOARDS.map((board, index) => (
                <a href={board.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                  <span>0{index + 1} · {board.kind}</span>
                  <h4>{board.title}</h4>
                  <p>{board.note}</p>
                  <strong>Open the 20-pin board ↗</strong>
                </a>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="sources" id="sources">
        <div class="sources__head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Reporting desk</p>
          <h2>Sources, boundaries, and the counts that move.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="source-list">
          {
            OYSTER_SOURCES.map((source, index) => (
              <a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <div>
                  <strong>{source.label}</strong>
                  <p>{source.use}</p>
                </div>
                <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b>
              </a>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <div class="boundary">
          <strong>Editorial boundary</strong>
          <p>
            Research checked July 29, 2026. Billion Oyster Project’s public pages can show figures from different
            update cycles; this edition uses the current homepage headline count and identifies older or
            project-specific measures in context. The El Segundo desk lists independent organizations as current
            places to learn from. No organization named here endorses PointCast or Beach Commons. No habitat work,
            event, volunteer shift, partnership, permit, contribution drive, municipal affiliation, or public
            program is announced.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <footer class="footer">
        <div>
          <a class="brand" href="/">PointCast</a>
          <p>Beach Commons V16 · Block 0543</p>
        </div>
        <p class="footer__line">The oysters are little. The institution is the reef.</p>
        <div class="footer__links">
          <a href="/beach-commons">All field studies</a>
          <a href="/beach-commons/v15">Previous: The Good Gear</a>
          <a href="/beach-commons/v16.json">Machine edition</a>
        </div>
      </footer>
    </main>

    <script>
      const radiusButtons = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-radius]'));
      const workButtons = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-work]'));
      const resources = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-resource]'));
      const status = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.filter-status');
      let activeRadius = 'all';
      let activeWork = 'all';

      const updateDesk = () => {
        let visible = 0;
        resources.forEach((resource) => {
          const radiusMatches = activeRadius === 'all' || resource.dataset.band === activeRadius;
          const workMatches =
            activeWork === 'all' || (resource.dataset.work || '').split(' ').includes(activeWork);
          const show = radiusMatches && workMatches;
          resource.hidden = !show;
          if (show) visible += 1;
        });
        if (status) status.textContent = `Showing ${visible} ${visible === 1 ? 'resource' : 'resources'}.`;
      };

      const selectButton = (buttons: HTMLButtonElement[], selected: HTMLButtonElement) => {
        buttons.forEach((button) => {
          const active = button === selected;
          button.classList.toggle('is-active', active);
          button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(active));
        });
      };

      radiusButtons.forEach((button) => {
        button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(button.classList.contains('is-active')));
        button.addEventListener('click', () => {
          activeRadius = button.dataset.radius || 'all';
          selectButton(radiusButtons, button);
          updateDesk();
        });
      });

      workButtons.forEach((button) => {
        button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(button.classList.contains('is-active')));
        button.addEventListener('click', () => {
          activeWork = button.dataset.work || 'all';
          selectButton(workButtons, button);
          updateDesk();
        });
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>

<style is:global>
  @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600;700&family=DM+Serif+Display:ital@0;1&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@500;600&display=swap');

  :root {
    --ink: #172c31;
    --deep: #163e48;
    --paper: #f4eddf;
    --paper-dark: #e5dac7;
    --oyster: #fffaf0;
    --red: #ed5b3a;
    --yellow: #f5cf58;
    --blue: #6ab8cc;
    --green: #7ca87f;
    --line: rgba(23, 44, 49, 0.25);
    --serif: 'DM Serif Display', Georgia, serif;
    --sans: 'DM Sans', Arial, sans-serif;
    --mono: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace;
  }

  * {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  html {
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
  }

  body {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink);
    background: var(--paper);
    font-family: var(--sans);
  }

  img {
    display: block;
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  a {
    color: inherit;
  }

  button {
    font: inherit;
  }

  .skip {
    position: fixed;
    left: 1rem;
    top: 1rem;
    z-index: 20;
    transform: translateY(-200%);
    padding: 0.7rem 1rem;
    color: var(--oyster);
    background: var(--deep);
  }

  .skip:focus {
    transform: translateY(0);
  }

  .nav {
    min-height: 3.5rem;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    font: 600 0.68rem/1.2 var(--mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .nav > span {
    text-align: center;
  }

  .nav > div {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  .nav a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .nav a:hover,
  .nav a:focus-visible {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.25rem;
  }

  .brand {
    font: 600 1rem/1 var(--mono);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.6fr) minmax(22rem, 0.8fr);
    min-height: calc(100vh - 3.5rem);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .hero__image {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 42rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--deep);
  }

  .hero__image img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .hero__stamp {
    position: absolute;
    left: 1rem;
    bottom: -0.2em;
    color: var(--yellow);
    font: 400 clamp(8rem, 20vw, 20rem)/0.75 var(--serif);
    text-shadow: 4px 4px 0 var(--red);
  }

  .hero__copy {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 6rem);
    background: var(--oyster);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font: 600 0.7rem/1.3 var(--mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
  }

  h1,
  h2,
  h3,
  p {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  h1 {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    font: 400 clamp(4.5rem, 8.5vw, 9rem)/0.82 var(--serif);
    letter-spacing: -0.055em;
  }

  h1 em {
    color: var(--red);
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .hero__dek {
    max-width: 28rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    font: 500 clamp(1.25rem, 2vw, 1.85rem)/1.14 var(--sans);
  }

  .byline {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.65rem;
